    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith">Ian Smith</a>'s cabinet declared independence on 11 November 1965 to forestall <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s policy of no independence before majority rule; no state ever recognised Rhodesia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> Resolution 216 called on all states to deny it recognition and assistance.</li><li>In 1976 the country held 270,000 white Rhodesians and six million black Africans, yet under the 1961 constitution whites held 50 of the 66 seats in the Assembly and the republican constitution of 1969 reserved the majority of seats for whites.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-national-union">ZANU</a>'s ZANLA and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-peoples-union">ZAPU</a>'s ZIPRA fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodesian-bush-war">Rhodesian Bush War</a> from the Sinoia engagement of April 1966; by 1978–79 defence took 47% of the budget, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancaster-house">Lancaster House</a> Agreement of late 1979 ended the rebellion.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>From 1975 to 1980 the Rhodesian security forces ran a chemical and biological warfare programme: they contaminated supplies planted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> caches and rural stores with parathion and thallium, poisoned water sources along infiltration routes, and used Vibrio cholerae, the agent of cholera.</p></aside>
    </section>

